                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 14.</seg></opener>
                <head>Mr. Mason to Earl Russell.—(Received July 30.)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>24 <hi rendition="#small-caps">Upper Seymour Street, Portman
                                Square,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-07-29"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >July</hi> 29, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Lord: As</hi> promised in my letter of the 21st of
                    July instant, I have now the honor to communicate herewith to your lordship a
                    copy of the despatch of the 6th of June ultimo, from the secretary of the
                    Confederate States to me, with copies of the documents accompanying it. They
                    relate to the matter of the dismissal of Mr. Moore, late British consul at
                    Richmond.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>J. M. MASON.</signed></closer>


                <postscript>
                    <p>P. S.—Since writing the above, I have received a letter from a Mr. Thomas
                        Kingsly Jones, a British subject, from Rankin county, Mississippi, who,
                        according to his own statement, has been most harshly dealt with, assaulted,
                        and one eye injured, and imprisoned as a malefactor at Jackson, in a
                        bitterly cold prison, for resisting the conscription. Mr. Jones holds a
                        certificate of British nationality, and he has already rendered military
                        service here, and has been honorably discharged, whose soldier’s discharge
                        is on file at my office.</p>
                    <p rendition="#right">G. M.</p>
                </postscript>






